From: xiaobao <xiaobao@local>
Date: Sat, 22 Nov 2026 14:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: rockchip: enable the CM3588 NAS PWM fan and retune its trips

The vendor rk3588-friendlyelec-cm3588-nas.dts ships the pwm-fan node with
status = "disabled", so on an Armbian vendor (rk-6.1-rkr7.2) image the fan
connector is dead: no /sys/class/hwmon pwm device, no cooling device, the 4
NVMe drives and the SoC have no forced airflow at all.

Enable it. &pwm1 is already status = "okay" (pwm1m1_pins) and fan-supply
resolves to vcc5v0_sys, which is regulator-always-on + regulator-boot-on in
rk3588s-nanopi-r6-common.dtsi, so the fan regulator is up before the driver
probes and there is no deferred-probe loop.

Retune rockchip,temp-trips from 50/55/60/65/70 to 45/50/55/60/65 degC.
On this kernel the trips are evaluated in-kernel: drivers/hwmon/pwm-fan.c
registers a rockchip_system_monitor notifier (CONFIG_ROCKCHIP_SYSTEM_MONITOR=y
in rockchip_linux_defconfig, and rk3588s.dtsi defines rockchip_system_monitor
with rockchip,thermal-zone = "soc-thermal"), so the table below is the whole
policy -- no userspace daemon needed.

The 45..65 window matches both community CM3588 fan controllers
(github.com/martabal/cm3588-fan: MIN_THRESHOLD=45, MAX_THRESHOLD=65, i.e.
state 1 at 45 up to state 5 above 65; github.com/vijaygill/pwm-fan-cm3588:
same 6-state ramp). The vendor 50..70 table was tuned for FriendlyElec's own
open chassis; in a closed NAS case with drives the package sits hotter and
the drives are the part that actually cooks.

cooling-levels are left exactly as the vendor set them (0 35 64 100 150 255,
i.e. duty = level/255: 0/13.7/25/39/59/100 percent). If the installed fan
stalls at state 1 the fix is to raise cooling-levels[1], NOT to move the
trips; check with:
    cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon*/name        # -> pwmfan
    echo 1 > .../pwm1; cat .../fan1_input  # or listen

Note: rockchip,hold-time-ms is not read by rk-6.1's pwm-fan driver (the
5.10-era vendor patch consumed it; drivers/hwmon/pwm-fan.c has no reference
to it). It is kept for parity with the vendor file. The effective debounce is
inside rockchip_system_monitor_thermal_update(): a falling edge is only
re-broadcast once the temperature dropped by more than 2000 millicelsius,
rising edges are broadcast every poll (rockchip,polling-delay defaults to
200 ms).

Also note: while rockchip,temp-trips is present and the system-monitor
notifier registers, the driver returns before
devm_thermal_of_cooling_device_register(), so there is deliberately NO
/sys/class/thermal/cooling_deviceN for the fan. That means the two
aforementioned daemons have nothing to write to. To use one of them instead
of the in-kernel table, delete the rockchip,temp-trips property; the driver
then falls through and registers the 6-state cooling device.

---
 .../dts/rockchip/rk3588-friendlyelec-cm3588-nas.dts  | 12 ++++++------
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3588-friendlyelec-cm3588-nas.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3588-friendlyelec-cm3588-nas.dts
index d2022f5..6dc5982 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3588-friendlyelec-cm3588-nas.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3588-friendlyelec-cm3588-nas.dts
@@ -76,7 +76,7 @@
 	};
 
 	fan: pwm-fan {
-		status = "disabled";
+		status = "okay";
 		compatible = "pwm-fan";
 		#cooling-cells = <2>;
 		fan-supply = <&vcc5v0_sys>;
@@ -84,11 +84,11 @@
 		cooling-levels = <0 35 64 100 150 255>;
 		rockchip,hold-time-ms = <2000>;
 		rockchip,temp-trips = <
-			50000	1
-			55000	2
-			60000	3
-			65000	4
-			70000	5
+			45000	1
+			50000	2
+			55000	3
+			60000	4
+			65000	5
 		>;
 	};
 
